<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  LAUNCH BACKGROUND — replaces the stretched splash.png bitmaps.

  ─── WHAT WAS WRONG ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Every splash.png in this project was a WHITE canvas (measured: 98.8% pure
  #FFFFFF pixels) carrying a small blue "X" that is not even our mark — it is
  the stock Capacitor placeholder that ships with `npx cap add android` and was
  never replaced.

  So on a black-themed crypto app the launch sequence was:
      white screen with a stranger's blue X  ->  black app
  which is exactly the "white box" complaint, and it is the most visible
  half-second the app has.

  ─── WHY A LAYER-LIST AND NOT A BITMAP ──────────────────────────────────────
  A single PNG set as android:windowBackground is STRETCHED to the window. The
  old files were fixed-aspect (480x320, 720x1280, ...), so on any phone whose
  aspect ratio did not match one of eleven hard-coded buckets the artwork was
  distorted. A layer-list draws a flat colour to the edges and centres the mark
  at its natural density size, so it is correct on every screen — foldables,
  tablets and split-screen included — from ONE definition.

  The colour is @color/ic_launcher_background (#00030F), the same value the
  adaptive launcher icon and Capacitor's backgroundColor use. Three places
  agreeing is what makes the icon -> splash -> app transition look continuous
  instead of like three different apps.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <item>
        <bitmap
            android:src="@drawable/splash_mark"
            android:gravity="center" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
